---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Sitrep from '../../components/Sitrep.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/vs/just-ask-your-agent/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/vs/just-ask-your-agent/',
    name: 'sitrep vs just asking your agent - re-derived answers or held state',
    description:
      'The most honest comparison on this site: "what is the status of this project?" typed into Claude Code, Cursor or Codex is free, needs no install, and often works. When the prompt is enough, where it stops scaling, and why the two are better together.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'What it is',
    sitrep: 'AI project status dashboard - a passive state layer over existing files',
    other: 'A capable agent re-reading your files and composing an answer on demand',
  },
  {
    dim: 'How the answer is made',
    sitrep: 'Extracted once by fixed conventions, kept current by a file watcher',
    other: 'Re-derived from scratch on every ask, by sampling a language model',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Time to an answer',
    sitrep: 'A glance - the state is already parsed when you look',
    other: 'The agent finds and reads the docs first, then summarizes - minutes, not moments',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Same question twice',
    sitrep: 'The same answer - a count moves only when a document changed',
    other: 'Two summaries, sometimes two different findings',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Provenance',
    sitrep: 'Every status, phase and decision keeps the file and line it came from',
    other: 'A composed summary; checking it means asking again or reading the docs yourself',
  },
  {
    dim: 'When the docs lie',
    sitrep: 'Staleness is measured - an “active” claim nothing has touched in weeks gets flagged against the repo’s real activity',
    other: 'The agent believes what the doc says unless told to cross-check dates, per doc, per ask',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Scope per ask',
    sitrep: 'Every project at once, newest activity first',
    other: 'In practice the repo the session is open in; “all my projects” means many asks',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Reads meaning',
    sitrep: 'No - it extracts what the docs state, it does not interpret prose',
    other: 'Yes - weighs contradictions, reads intent, answers follow-ups. Genuinely better at this',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Writes to your files',
    sitrep: 'Three guarded state operations in the document’s own convention; nothing else, ever',
    other: '“Make sure the docs are up to date” hands the whole record to free-form edits',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Cost per glance',
    sitrep: 'Nothing - a local parse you look at, no tokens spent',
    other: 'Tokens and minutes, every time the question is asked',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sitrep vs just asking your agent - re-derived answers or held state"
  description="The realest alternative to sitrep is not another tool - it is typing 'what is the status?' into Claude Code, Cursor or Codex. This page is honest about when that prompt is enough, where it stops scaling, and why the prompt gets better with sitrep underneath it."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        <Sitrep /> vs just asking your agent
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        The realest alternative to <Sitrep /> is not any tool on these pages. It
        is typing "what is the status of this project?" into the agent you
        already run. That prompt is free, needs no install, and inside one
        warm repo it genuinely works - so this page starts by conceding it,
        and then is precise about where it stops.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Disclosure: this page is written by <a href="/about/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">the maker of sitrep</a>. Read the
        framing with that in mind - and note that where the honest answer is
        "just ask", this page says so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="difference">
      <h2 id="difference" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The one-sentence difference</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          The prompt re-derives state; <Sitrep /> holds it. Every time you ask,
          the agent has to find the record before it can judge it - locate
          the plans, the decision logs, the progress notes, read them, and
          compose an answer. That work is re-paid on every ask, the answer
          arrives minutes later as a paragraph, and the paragraph reflects
          that one session's reading. Ask again tomorrow and you pay again -
          and may get a different paragraph.
        </p>
        <p>
          <Sitrep /> pays the reading cost once, at parse time, and keeps the
          result current as files change. Status, phases, open decisions,
          due dates and staleness are extracted by fixed conventions, so the
          same question gets the same answer twice, every count moves only
          when a document moved, and every extracted item keeps the file and
          line it came from. The glance costs nothing, which is what lets it
          happen twenty times a day.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the prompt has that <Sitrep /> never will: judgment. An agent
          reads meaning - it can notice that a doc marked active declares
          its own approach dead in the prose, weigh contradictions, answer
          follow-ups. <Sitrep /> deliberately refuses to interpret prose,
          because an extractor that guesses at meaning invents findings.
          One is an instrument, the other is an analyst.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="table">
      <h2 id="table" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"><Sitrep /></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium">Asking your agent</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ dim, sitrep, other }) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium">{dim}</th>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted">{sitrep}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-ink-muted">{other}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-prompt">
      <h2 id="choose-prompt" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to just ask</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          One repo, and you are actively in it. The context is warm, the
          agent is already there, and its answer benefits from everything
          the session has seen.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          The question needs interpretation, not state: "is this approach
          still viable?", "does this plan contradict the new requirement?" -
          judgment calls an extractor should never make.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want a conversation - follow-ups, "why", "what should I do
          first" - not a dashboard.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Nothing to install, nothing to buy: you already pay for the agent.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-sitrep">
      <h2 id="choose-sitrep" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose <Sitrep /></h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          <span>
            The question is plural: where do <em>all</em> the projects stand,
            what moved since last week, what is waiting on you across every
            repo - answered at a glance instead of one session per project.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Re-entry is the pain: opening a cold project after weeks away.
          The minutes the prompt spends re-reading the record before it can
          answer are exactly the cold open you are trying to skip.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          The numbers have to be trustworthy: counts that change only when
          files change, and every claim traceable to the line it was parsed
          from - not a fresh composition each time, with no way to tell a
          real change from a different reading.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          <span>
            You care whether the docs still tell the truth. A status line is a
            claim about a moment; <Sitrep /> checks claims against the repo's
            actual activity and flags the ones going stale - arithmetic the
            prompt would have to redo across every project, every ask.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          <span>
            You do not want "keep the docs up to date" to mean unsupervised
            free-form edits to your project record. <Sitrep />'s writes are
            three guarded state operations, previewed, in the document's own
            convention - and nothing else, ever.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="both">
      <h2 id="both" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Using both</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          This is the pairing the comparison actually resolves to: the
          prompt is not <Sitrep />'s competitor so much as its best client.
          <Sitrep /> ships an MCP server that hands agents its parsed state,
          read-only - so "what is waiting on me?" stops being a
          re-derivation and becomes a lookup, and the agent spends its
          context on judgment instead of discovery.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same split rescues the bigger prompt. "Review recent changes
          and bring the docs up to date" run bare must first reconstruct an
          unverified, single-use picture of where everything stands - then
          throw it away. Run with <Sitrep /> underneath, the stale claims, due
          items and open decisions arrive as a worklist with file and line;
          the agent applies its judgment and makes the edits in your
          session, where you see them; and the dashboard afterwards shows
          whether the stale count actually dropped. The analyst works from
          the instrument's readings - and the instrument checks the
          analyst's work.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink-faint">
          <Sitrep /> is in development, launching first on macOS. Local-first -
          your files never leave your machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
